00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme the government's  grand plan for reducing emissions from  planes trains and automobiles is  published  find out what the future looks like for  how we get around  a warning the amazon rainforest could be  starting to emit  more carbon dioxide than it absorbs  and kicking off a summer of  sustainability on sky sports  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and the government says it's  the world's  first green print an ambitious plan to  decarbonize  all forms of domestic transport by 2050.  the critics argue the proposals are  unrealistic  underfunded and the technology to meet  the targets doesn't exist yet  today the uk's transport decarbonisation  plan was published  our climate correspondent hannah thomas  peter has all the details on what it  will mean  for how we move around green energy it's  it's our future yeah we can't rely on  coal  anymore or diesels  thomas has been driving trucks for a  decade he knows that transport is the  uk's largest contributor to emissions  and agrees that needs to change but as  part of its drive to decarbonize the  sector  the government has announced there'll be  no new diesel hgv  sold after 2040 a big change  at an uncertain time the plan has left  an industry  still grappling with hydrogen and  electricity as alternatives  feeling adrift this proposal is  unrealistic alternative hgvs  don't yet exist we don't know when  they'll exist  and we don't know how much they'll cost  and it's not clear what any  transition will look like  trucking isn't the only industry  affected by new targets  the department for transport is also  pledging to get domestic aviation  carbon emissions down to net zero by  2040  that's ten years earlier than previously  promised  but the decarbonization of our world  isn't straight  forward and when it comes to planes for  example the technology used to make them  greener  is still very much in development and  it's expensive  in fact money seems to be the key issue  here the government criticized for  producing policy  without a plan yet to help pay for it  unless we see action now from the  treasury  and from richie sunak the decarbonising  transport plan and any plan on climate  that comes over the coming weeks won't  deliver what it needs to  that may be true but this roadmap  contains  a series of big gambles that the markets  consumers technology and the  government's own purse string holders  will all deliver as we march towards net  zero  hannah thomas peter sky news heathrow  in today's other climate news nasa has  warned the moon's  wobbly orbit around earth could impact  global coastlines  because the moon's gravity pulls the  ocean's tides towards it  this wobble set to start by 2035  will cause higher tides and higher sea  levels  combined with rising sea levels caused  by climate change some countries will  see a dramatic  increase in flooding the european union  has unveiled  new laws to help meet its target to cut  greenhouse gas emissions by 55 percent  before the end of the decade a dozen  major proposals  include phasing out the sale of petrol  cars and plans to tax the carbon  emissions generated by goods imported  from foreign countries  it's now down to the 27 member countries  and eu parliament  to agree to the proposals and singapore  has built one of the world's  largest floating solar panel farms  spanning an area the size of 45 football  fields the panels will produce enough  electricity to power the island's five  water treatment plants  the solar farm will reduce carbon  emissions by about 32  000 tons every year the equivalent to 7  000 cars being taken off the roads  now here on our data dashboard you can  see the total amount of carbon dioxide  the world has produced in million tons  it's ticking up the whole time as fossil  fuels such as coal  oil and gas are burned now the world has  long relied on the amazon  to help absorb some of these emissions  but now researchers are warning  the rainforest's ability to act as the  earth's lungs  could be changing well our science  correspondent thomas moore  is here to explain why thomas global  warming  would be a whole lot worse if it wasn't  for the world's plants  sucking up carbon dioxide since 1960  they've absorbed around  a quarter of all fossil fuel emissions  and the amazon has been a big part of  that  it contains an estimated 123 billion  tons of carbon  but new research shows the amazon basin  is in trouble  in the west it's absorbing slightly more  carbon than is being released from dead  rotting trees and human impact  but in the south and the east which  accounts for more than a quarter of the  amazon area it's become  a net source of carbon so how's that  happened  well the amazon gets more than two  meters of rain  on average every year between a quarter  and a third of that results from  moisture  that's released by trees but the  amazon's climate is changing  particularly in the east over the last  40 years  rainfall at times of the year has  decreased by up to a third  and temperatures have increased by up to  two and a half degrees  now the scientists say there is a clear  link with deforestation  in the east around a quarter of the  amazon has been cleared  mostly for agriculture more than twice  as much as the west  the fires used to clear the forest  release huge amounts of carbon but the  absence of trees also  dries out the surrounding forest raising  the risk of  more fires as well as stunting the  growth of the remaining trees and  reducing carbon uptake  it could be the beginning of a major  tipping point where in just a few  decades  much of the forest dies that would  shrink the habitat of unique species  and accelerate global warming thomas  thank you  now free public transport and a scheme  to help households switch to green  alternatives  could help make the transition to a net  zero future fairer for everyone  now that's according to the  environmental justice commission which  wants people to be put  at the heart of the government's climate  targets their findings were informed by  four citizens juries who suggested  changes they thought would help them go  green  the biggest barrier is our lifestyle  is cost i think the biggest thing all  around was  obviously education when i joined the  panel  i knew very little about it it was a  pretty big eye opener to me  those of us who are our jurors on the  program could act as colombia champions  in our neighborhood in the local areas  so we could then pass the message on to  the local communities our demands  fuel what is produced why do we operate  like this  can we do it differently we are going  against  years and years of conditioning  people have over the years developed  habits  fast fashion fast foods fast everything  there should be more information out  there  for people to to realize what's going on  there's a need to  engage with the local communities a bit  more  well luke murphy is the head of the  environmental justice commission and  joins me now  so your report says that people and  fairness need to be put at the heart of  the drive to hit net zero so what does  that mean and how can it be achieved  well firstly i mean we know that action  on the climate and nature crises  is urgent the question now is really  about how we carry out that transition  and the crucial message of the report  today that we launched today  is the opportunity to improve people's  quality of life  there's a real opportunity to do that  through this transition in creating new  jobs improving health and well-being  and tackling poverty but what we heard  from people and communities right across  the country  was that this transition must be rooted  in fairness because unless it is rooted  in fairness  it simply won't succeed because the  public support for the transition  simply won't be there and the government  has set out its strategy for for zero  carbon transport does that  meet your brief of putting people in  fairness first  i don't think it does i mean we  obviously welcome some of the measures  it's it  has some ambitious proposals in terms of  uh an earlier phase out  of petrol and diesel vehicles and it's a  particular focus on trucks  but i think what we've called for and  actually what we heard from  citizens across the country from south  wales valleys  was that they want to see much greater  investment in affordable  accessible public transport they want to  see greater investment  in active transport in walking and  cycling and that really was missing from  the government's strategy today lots of  targets lots of lofty ambitions  but i'm not sure there's really enough  to deliver the kind of change  that actually the kind of the the people  want to see across the country  and you've listened to people as you say  up and down the land do you think that  the public should play a greater  role in formulating climate-related  policies  absolutely that's something we we heard  again and again  people want three things they want more  information they think  actually there should be a kind of uh an  information campaign similar to covid  that recognizes the scale of the threat  of climate change and the scale of the  change that we need to  undertake secondly they want to be more  involved in decisions themselves they  want to see  more things like juries that go beyond  just  the normal consultation and thirdly they  want to see more powers  devolved and given away from westminster  uh or the devolved governments to their  local areas to their local councils  no plan um we can't have  one-size-fits-all  plans that's what we were told and so we  need to see powers distributed across  the country  to allow local areas to develop their  own plans  luke murphy thank you thank you  now this summer sky sports is  highlighting the impact of climate  change at some of the uk's highest  profile sporting events  ahead of the cop 26 summit in glasgow in  november  and to kick it all off sky sports news  presenter david garrido  has hit the road in an electric car of  course  right stop one hot's the way tottenham's  training ground we're here with club  ambassador deadly king  you're top of the sustainability premier  league table why should fans care  climate change is something that is  affecting us all when it's failed that  we will do our part  as we are doing here at the club okay  listen lads i'd love to chat and stay up  for breakfast but we're on an electric  road trip let's go  stop two is the overall we are talking  the first ever prototype bamboo cricket  bat ben is the co-inventor talk to me  about this ben  so it captures more conductors than a  willow cricket bat it's grown next to  the factories so we save on transport  missions  and it's got a better end of life than a  willow cricket bat  stop three is dorney lake i'm here with  some gb rowers the 1992 champion greg  sterl  and melissa wilson as well melissa i've  been hearing about this as a symbol as a  gesture what does that mean exactly  yeah we're creating a green legacy for  team gb this summer and part of that is  involving athletes too  so we've got them doing this symbol it's  the olympic rings it's planet earth it's  the need to get to carbon zero and  you'll be seeing it on your screens this  well that's everything from us for today  coming up tomorrow the people  forced from their homes by extreme  weather events becoming more common  and more frequent because of climate  change you can see that here at the same  time on sky news  thanks for watching see you then

